this traditional water mill can grind  over 550 pounds of flour in a single day  this is the logo show what a meal of  wielded down a museum in singleton West  Sussex  the way we make the flour is exactly the  same way as it's been made for  generations it's the week there's him at  the top the flour comes out at the  bottom we don't have anything in at all  we don't take anything else the logo  show meal lates the 17th century but  despite being over 400 years old he  hasn't always been in the same location  its original site was the lager show  village in West Sussex which is named  after the original mill had two water  wheels in tandem each will travel its  own independent set of machinery with  time only the tailwater will remained in  use and the cast iron will replace the  wooden one the lager sawmill was  incorporated into the museum in 1973 the  museum carried out restoration works of  the machinery and the interior but he  was still lacking something essential  the problem was but on this site we  don't have a river so we have to  engineer of a water system dig out a big  pond unlined it because underneath this  chalk which absorbs water and then below  the wheel we need another pond to  collect the water that had to be dug out  and lined  and the coasters my river we have to  pump that water back up and reuse it  nowadays the nail is used for grinding  corn flour and animal feed  it's applies to more Baker's and farm  shops around the singleton village we  follow the journey of a bag of wheat as  it is milled into flour the mill is  structured over three floors the top  floor where grain is fed from the first  floor or stone floor where the grinding  takes place and the ground floor or spot  floor where horizontal shaft connects  the waterwheel to the machinery the  Mills driven by water and so we need to  have water flowing over the waterwheel  we start the mill by taking a very large  spammer up to the sluice gates which  control where the water comes out of the  big mill point we open the sluice gate  water then flows along a channel at the  back of the mill until it reaches the  waterwheel and it starts to fill up what  we call buckets they don't look like the  bucket that you would use for cleaning  the floor or something like that but  they each slot contains water and we  need to fill about four of those before  the weight of the water makes the wheel  turn and then the water tops up the next  one and the next one so it keeps turning  continuously the mill is 12 feet in  diameter when grinding it turns at about  6 revolutions per minute each complete  turn is powered by 200 gallons of water  when we need to mill we take the bag  into the middle and we use a chain hoist  to lift it up to the top floor of the  mill  [Music]  we then open the bag sometimes the  string will pull sometimes we have to  use scissors and we then pour it down  the chute into a hopper above the  machinery  [Music]  in this big barrel there are two stones  just like these miniature ones the  bottom one called the bad stone that  doesn't move the top one called the  runner stone is turned by the machinery  and it's the one that is actually doing  the grinding we can control the grade of  the flour by the amount of grain that we  put in and the distance apart of the  stones and everything is checked by  what's known as the rule of thumb with  no gauges no machinery we just use our  thumb and our forefinger and if it feels  right it is right over all the stones  rotate thirteen and a half times each  rotation of the waterwheel at an average  speed of 80 revolutions per minute I  enjoy the fact that when I come to the  museum at the end of the day I can look  at a ton of fans as I help to make that  and because we are also a museum and we  have visitors it's fascinating to talk  to the visitors and some of them tell me  stories about how they've seen a mill  being used when they were children and  get people who come from various parts  of 